    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d228">

                <pb facs="0332" n="230" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg230"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/6013: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan (Grew) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-03-10T18:00:00+09:00">March 10, 1933—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 10—7:24 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>57. Press correspondents quote Foreign Office spokesman to the effect that the
                    Japanese Legation has been instructed to approach Chang Hsueh-liang in Peiping
                    to induce him to withdraw his forces from the neighborhood of Kupeikow, or to
                    create a neutral zone south of the Wall failing which the Japanese threaten to
                    move into North China in force.</p>
                <p>The Military Attaché informs me that the General Staff state that there is some
                    fighting at Kupeikow, where the Great Wall makes a loop; that the Japanese hold
                    the Wall and that there are Chinese troops inside and outside the pass; that the
                    Japanese have insisted that the Chinese withdraw from this area and that it may
                    be necessary for the Japanese to send a detachment down from the Wall on either
                    side of the Chinese force to compel withdrawal; that the Japanese have no
                    further intentions unless the Chinese at the Wall are supported by new
                    troops.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
